---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { AdSlot } from '../components/ui/AdSlot';

const title = 'Calculation Methods & Assumptions | Contractor Ledger';
const description = 'Formulas used by Contractor Ledger for gross margin, labor cost, monthly break-even, and material waste calculations, with key assumptions.';
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} canonicalPath="/methodology/">
  <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 py-10 lg:py-14 space-y-10">
    <!-- Breadcrumbs -->
    <nav class="flex items-center gap-2 text-xs text-text-muted" aria-label="Breadcrumbs">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-text-primary transition-colors">Home</a>
      <span>/</span>
      <span class="text-text-primary font-semibold">Methodology</span>
    </nav>

    <div class="space-y-3">
      <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-extrabold text-text-primary tracking-tight font-headline">
        Calculation Methods &amp; Assumptions
      </h1>
      <p class="text-base text-text-secondary leading-relaxed">
        These formulas describe how the calculators combine the values you enter. Results depend on those inputs and do not verify local wages, tax rules, insurance rates, or contract requirements.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Section 1: Gross Margin Divisor -->
    <article class="bg-surface rounded-xl border border-border-default p-6 sm:p-8 shadow-subtle space-y-4">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-text-primary font-headline">
        1. Gross Margin Divisor Arithmetic
      </h2>
      <div class="space-y-3 text-sm text-text-secondary leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          In contractor accounting, gross profit margin represents the share of total contract revenue that remains after satisfying all direct job expenses and allocated overhead. It is governed by:
        </p>
        <div class="p-4 rounded-lg bg-canvas border border-border-default font-mono text-xs text-text-primary space-y-1.5">
          <div class="font-bold text-brand-copper">Selling Price = Total Estimated Cost ÷ (1 − Target Margin % ÷ 100)</div>
          <div>Gross Profit = Selling Price − Total Estimated Cost</div>
          <div>Actual Gross Margin (%) = (Gross Profit ÷ Selling Price) × 100</div>
        </div>
        <p>
          <strong>Margin versus markup:</strong> Multiplying a $1,000 cost by 1.25 produces a selling price of $1,250 with a $250 profit. The resulting margin is <strong>20.0%</strong> because ($250 ÷ $1,250) × 100 = 20%; a 25% target margin instead divides total cost by 0.75. The job pricing and project estimate calculators use this margin divisor.
        </p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- Section 2: Loaded Labor Burden -->
    <article class="bg-surface rounded-xl border border-border-default p-6 sm:p-8 shadow-subtle space-y-4">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-text-primary font-headline">
        2. Loaded Labor Cost Formula
      </h2>
      <div class="space-y-3 text-sm text-text-secondary leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          Direct wages represent only the visible portion of field personnel expenses. Contractor Ledger calculates fully burdened hourly costs using the formula:
        </p>
        <div class="p-4 rounded-lg bg-canvas border border-border-default font-mono text-xs text-text-primary space-y-1.5">
          <div>Regular Wages = Crew Count × Regular Hours × Hourly Wage</div>
          <div>Overtime Wages = Crew Count × Overtime Hours × Hourly Wage × OT Multiplier</div>
          <div>Gross Wages = Regular Wages + Overtime Wages</div>
          <div>Employer Burden = Gross Wages × Burden Percentage</div>
          <div class="font-bold text-brand-copper">Total Labor Cost = Gross Wages + Employer Burden + Additional Expenses</div>
          <div class="font-bold text-brand-copper">Loaded Hourly Rate = Total Labor Cost ÷ Total Crew Hours</div>
        </div>
        <p>
          The burden percentage is user-entered. Payroll taxes, unemployment insurance, workers' compensation, and overtime requirements vary with current rules and employer-specific facts, so verify the values that apply to your business.
        </p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- Section 3: Break-Even Recovery -->
    <article class="bg-surface rounded-xl border border-border-default p-6 sm:p-8 shadow-subtle space-y-4">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-text-primary font-headline">
        3. Break-Even Rate &amp; Capacity Recovery
      </h2>
      <div class="space-y-3 text-sm text-text-secondary leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          To prevent overhead deficits, monthly fixed operating expenses must be amortized over realistic billable production hours:
        </p>
        <div class="p-4 rounded-lg bg-canvas border border-border-default font-mono text-xs text-text-primary space-y-1.5">
          <div>Fixed Overhead Recovery / Hr = Monthly Fixed Costs ÷ Monthly Billable Hours</div>
          <div class="font-bold text-brand-copper">Break-Even Rate = Fixed Overhead Recovery / Hr + Variable Cost / Hr</div>
          <div class="font-bold text-brand-copper">Required Monthly Revenue = Fixed Costs + (Variable Cost / Hr × Billable Hours)</div>
        </div>
        <p>
          A positive billable hour volume is mathematically required to compute a finite hourly rate.
        </p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- Section 4: Material Waste & Procurement -->
    <article class="bg-surface rounded-xl border border-border-default p-6 sm:p-8 shadow-subtle space-y-4">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-text-primary font-headline">
        4. Material Waste &amp; Procurement Buffer
      </h2>
      <div class="space-y-3 text-sm text-text-secondary leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          Material estimating requires accounting for scrap, dimensional cutting losses, and transit damage before applying procurement markup:
        </p>
        <div class="p-4 rounded-lg bg-canvas border border-border-default font-mono text-xs text-text-primary space-y-1.5">
          <div>Effective Quantity = Net Specified Quantity × (1 + Waste Allowance % ÷ 100)</div>
          <div>Adjusted Procurement Cost = Effective Quantity × Unit Cost</div>
          <div>Markup Amount = Adjusted Procurement Cost × Markup % ÷ 100</div>
          <div class="font-bold text-brand-copper">Billed Material Price = Adjusted Procurement Cost + Markup Amount</div>
        </div>
        <p>
          Quantities support precise floating-point decimals to allow fractional procurement for aggregate, linear footage, and yardage.
        </p>
        <p>
          Currency inputs and project line items are rounded to cents before totals are summed. Material quantities preserve up to four decimal places; other displayed quantities may be rounded by their calculator's documented output precision.
        </p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <!-- AdSense Placement -->
    <AdSlot slotId="methodology-banner" />
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
